1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:10,756
In this video, we'll set up the AIFS prototype — ckptfs — going from a clean Ubuntu machine to a working, accelerated checkpoint mount.

2
00:00:11,306 --> 00:00:20,498
By the end, you'll have built the filesystem from source, mounted it, written a real checkpoint through it, and watched it replicate to durable storage.

3
00:00:20,998 --> 00:00:23,854
Plus — what to do when something goes wrong.

4
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:28,428
Before we install anything — the mental model.

5
00:00:29,028 --> 00:00:32,004
ckptfs takes three directories.

6
00:00:32,504 --> 00:00:38,288
The mountpoint is the virtual filesystem your applications see. Nothing is actually stored there.

7
00:00:38,838 --> 00:00:44,478
The spool is fast, local NVMe — every new write lands there first.

8
00:00:45,028 --> 00:00:53,860
And the lower directory is your durable storage — an NFS share, in production — which remains the source of truth after a crash.

9
00:00:54,460 --> 00:00:58,060
One binary, three paths. That's the whole system.

10
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:07,524
You'll need a Linux machine — Ubuntu 22.04 or later, or WSL2 on Windows — with sudo access.

11
00:01:08,024 --> 00:01:17,528
The toolchain is just four packages: build-essential for the C compiler, fuse3, the libfuse3 development headers, and pkg-config.

12
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:23,184
We start with apt-get update, then install the four packages.

13
00:01:23,684 --> 00:01:26,684
On a fresh machine, this takes a minute or two.

14
00:01:27,134 --> 00:01:34,838
When it finishes, verify that GCC is available — version 13 here, on Ubuntu 24.04.

15
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:41,636
Next, clone the repository from GitHub, change into it, and run make.

16
00:01:42,136 --> 00:01:48,232
The build takes a few seconds — and produces a single, small binary: ckptfs.

17
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:57,940
Now create the three directories — the mountpoint, the durable backend, and the NVMe spool — and take ownership of them.

18
00:01:58,490 --> 00:02:02,762
Then launch ckptfs with the three paths, in that order.

19
00:02:03,262 --> 00:02:08,758
It runs in the foreground — so keep this terminal open, and do your work in a second one.

20
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:13,736
From that second terminal, confirm the mount is live.

21
00:02:14,236 --> 00:02:21,364
Then write a test file through it with dd. Notice the speed — that write landed on the local spool immediately.

22
00:02:21,914 --> 00:02:29,258
Moments later, the same file appears in the durable backend — together with a hidden marker: .aifs_committed.

23
00:02:29,758 --> 00:02:34,150
That marker means the whole checkpoint directory is safely replicated.

24
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:37,768
Now — the real workflow.

25
00:02:38,318 --> 00:02:48,926
We save a multi-file training checkpoint — model, optimizer, scheduler, RNG state — while refreshing both directory listings five times a second.

26
00:02:49,526 --> 00:02:54,590
The spool on the left fills instantly. A training job would already be unblocked.

27
00:02:55,240 --> 00:03:04,048
On the right, the background replicator copies each file over, in order — and the commit marker arrives last, sealing the checkpoint.

28
00:03:04,648 --> 00:03:11,272
On real hardware with an NFS backend, that right-hand column trails by thirty to forty seconds.

29
00:03:11,772 --> 00:03:14,052
And the GPUs never wait for it.

30
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:17,476
So — what about crashes?

31
00:03:17,976 --> 00:03:21,648
We kill ckptfs mid-flight, and restart it.

32
00:03:22,198 --> 00:03:27,238
On startup, the old spool is quarantined — never trusted after a crash.

33
00:03:27,738 --> 00:03:31,626
But the durable backend still holds every committed checkpoint.

34
00:03:32,126 --> 00:03:38,702
The recovery rule is simple: resume training from the newest checkpoint that carries the commit marker.

35
00:03:40,300 --> 00:03:45,628
One note — this demo ran in a container, so both tiers shared a single disk.

36
00:03:46,128 --> 00:03:55,344
On a real cloud VM, you'd first provision the local NVMe disk: partition it with fdisk, format it ext4, and mount it.

37
00:03:55,844 --> 00:04:01,652
The full transcript is in the README, recorded on an Azure L8as_v3.

38
00:04:02,202 --> 00:04:12,306
One warning before you use the bundled setup script: it force-formats the first NVMe device it finds — and on many VMs, that's the OS disk.

39
00:04:12,806 --> 00:04:15,110
Always check the device name first.

40
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:25,416
Set up this way on real hardware, the prototype sustained 879 megabytes per second, on the fully durable path.

41
00:04:25,916 --> 00:04:35,660
That's a four-gibibyte checkpoint, locally safe in under five seconds — with replication completing off the critical path, about half a minute later.

42
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:40,228
Finally — the issues you're most likely to hit.

43
00:04:40,778 --> 00:04:47,330
If the mount is dead after a crash, unmount it with fusermount3 -u before relaunching.

44
00:04:47,830 --> 00:04:56,134
If ls on the mount shows nothing after a restart — that's a known prototype limitation. Your data is still safe, in the backend.

45
00:04:56,684 --> 00:05:03,236
Write checkpoints into their own directories — never loose files at the mount root — or commit markers won't work.

46
00:05:03,736 --> 00:05:09,832
And clean up quarantined orphan spool directories from time to time. They're never deleted automatically.

47
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:17,428
And that's the whole setup. Four packages, one make, three directories, one command.

48
00:05:18,028 --> 00:05:23,644
The full install guide and troubleshooting notes live in the repository, under deliverables.

49
00:05:24,144 --> 00:05:28,704
Try it against your own training pipeline — and stop paying the checkpoint tax.

